artner:52576 BRANCH=none TEST=make buildall; try on Cr50  I manually tested both highsec and highperf variants, as well as forcing the bootrom init to run. All the bank registers were loaded with meaningful values, and none of the SPI or USB functionality showed any problems.  Change-Id: Ia91ba98ef4c667aec74195c4a7bbf72a5d1c8b2d Signed-off-by: Bill Richardson &lt;wfrichar@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/342030 Reviewed-by: Vadim Bendebury &lt;vbendeb@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="342030" owner_email="wfrichar@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/30/342030/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I87aec168dcac406acd00091dd55a6492a9b9c18e" commit="78ac01b84f3bee3641c5cb657d73b958c06e8eea" commit_message="Also upload VM images.  BUG=chromium:591624 TEST=trybots  Change-Id: I87aec168dcac406acd00091dd55a6492a9b9c18e Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/342040 Commit-Ready: Achuith Bhandarkar &lt;achuith@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Achuith Bhandarkar &lt;achuith@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Aviv Keshet &lt;akeshet@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="342040" owner_email="achuith@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/chromite" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/chromite" ref="refs/changes/40/342040/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I86495211dd48a8c9f19cd21c00b16fc678a2c656" commit="2ff8d1e704dbae0014a6c2386bd3f4a65657d6ae" commit_message="chipset-apl: Change default kernel to 4.4 for APL  The 4.4 kernel is the target kernel for all Appolo Lake platforms.  BUG=None TEST=None  Change-Id: I86495211dd48a8c9f19cd21c00b16fc678a2c656 Signed-off-by: Brian J Lovin &lt;brian.j.lovin@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/339694 Commit-Ready: Wayne Boyer &lt;wayne.boyer@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Wayne Boyer &lt;wayne.boyer@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Wayne Boyer &lt;wayne.boyer@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Bernie Thompson &lt;bhthompson@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="339694" owner_email="brian.j.lovin@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/overlays/board-overlays" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/board-overlays" ref="refs/changes/94/339694/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I6666c4382ef40d1211995101c97a52346963fa4a" commit="7929e8d3041cf65ff4021e546daa70a6d183042f" commit_message="cros_ec: Change EC interface to be fully object-oriented  Depthcharge's current cros_ec code doesn't really match the object-oriented one-instance-per-device design of its other drivers. Instead, it is a singleton design with hardcoded support for only up to one additional chip by passing devidx=1 to certain functions. In addition, the vboot callback implementations for EC software sync are tightly interwoven with the cros_ec driver.  This patch changes that code to be more in line with depthcharge's other drivers. EC and PD chips are represented as separate objects (sharing the same bus object), and the connection to vboot callbacks is abstracted in a VbootEcOps superclass. This is intended to make it easier to hook up other embedded controllers that don't run the Chrome EC embedded OS to vboot's software sync mechanism, such as the ANX7688 used on Elm. (This is intended to be the first step. We may or may not decide that it makes sense to also make changes to vboot code and the callback interface later.)  Also try to clean up namespacing a bit (cros_ec_... only for exported functions), only export functions that are really needed by other files, and remove some unused code.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:52434 TEST=FAFT on Oak. Manually triggered software 